sub:assertion {
  <https://w3id.org/delphi/resource/approach/trabecular-stress-alignment-wolffs-law--grok-4-7--3> a prov:Plan, delphi:TestableHypothesis ;
    rdfs:label "Lean: shear vanishes only on principal planes" ;
    dct:subject <https://w3id.org/delphi/resource/problem/trabecular-stress-alignment-wolffs-law> ;
    dct:description "Thompson's reason for alignment, in thompson1917-ch16-06, is that a plane along a principal direction carries no shear. The repo already proves that principal directions of a 2D symmetric stress are orthogonal (ThompsonLaws.Mechanics.principalDirections_orthogonal). Extend that file. For the stress [[a, b], [b, c]] and a unit normal, define the shear component of the traction and prove it is zero if and only if the normal is an eigenvector, when the two principal values are distinct. Then prove the alignment lemma used as the optimality piece: for a fixed stress with distinct eigenvalues, the Frobenius product with a fabric of fixed eigenvalues is maximised exactly when the eigenframes coincide. In 2D that product is a cos 2θ + b sin 2θ, and the maximum is at θ = 0. The development stays in 2D plane stress so it can close on the existing lemma. It does not say that bone does this." ;

    delphi:proposedTest "A Lean module, compiled with no sorry, containing both the shear characterisation and the 2D alignment-maximum theorem." ;
    delphi:context "Fails if: Either theorem still contains sorry, or the alignment statement is true only after assuming something the mechanics lemma does not give (a nonsymmetric stress, or a 3D spectral theorem the development does not have)." ;
    delphi:hypothesisType "approach/formalization" ;
    skos:note "Steps: 1. Define traction and its shear part for a 2D symmetric stress. 2. Prove shear vanishes if and only if the normal is a principal direction. 3. Prove the 2D Frobenius product is maximised when the frames coincide. 4. Build the Thompson Lake project with no sorry and only standard axioms." ;
    skos:note "Difficulty (proposer's estimate of completion within about a month of agent time): tractable" ;
    skos:note "Tool: lean4" ;
    skos:related <https://w3id.org/delphi/resource/claim/thompson1917-ch16-05> ;
    skos:related <https://w3id.org/delphi/resource/claim/thompson1917-ch16-06> ;
    skos:related <https://w3id.org/delphi/resource/claim/thompson1917-ch16-07> ;
    skos:related <https://w3id.org/delphi/resource/claim/thompson1917-ch16-16> ;


    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://github.com/fractastical/delphi/blob/ee98025a1960b300fcc06aa2106f51118b60f565/problems/approaches/grok-4-7.json> .
  <https://w3id.org/delphi/resource/problem/trabecular-stress-alignment-wolffs-law> a delphi:Concept, schema:Question ;
    rdfs:label "Do trabeculae align with principal stress, and is that alignment causal?" .



}

